window.SPARKWA_SEARCH=[{"title":"Documentation","description":"Browse SparkWA installation and operation guides.","path":"docs/","body":"Documentation guides overview install WhatsApp Spark Gmail OAuth security"},{"title":"Install on a VPS","description":"Deploy SparkWA, pair WhatsApp, and run it as a service.","path":"docs/vps-install/","body":" Ubuntu VPS Installation & Deployment Guide This guide describes the complete workflow to deploy SparkWA to a headless Ubuntu VPS Ubuntu 24.04 LTS / 22.04 LTS / Debian . The application runs headlessly as a native background service using systemd. --- 1. System Requirements - Operating System: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS or 22.04 LTS x86 64 or aarch64 . - Compute: 1 vCPU, 512 MB to 1 GB RAM. - Disk: 50 MB free disk space. - Network: Direct outbound HTTPS port 443 and WhatsApp TCP ports 443 / 5222 . - Runtime: Zero dependencies No Node.js, Python, Docker, Chromium, or desktop required . --- 2. Step 1: Upload Archive to VPS From your local machine where you built or downloaded the release archive: bash Upload release archive and credentials JSON to your VPS scp sparkwa-v1.0.0-linux-amd64.tar.gz user@your-vps-ip:/tmp/ scp credentials.json user@your-vps-ip:/tmp/ --- 3. Step 2: SSH into VPS and Extract bash ssh user@your-vps-ip Navigate to /tmp and extract package cd /tmp tar -xzf sparkwa-v1.0.0-linux-amd64.tar.gz cd sparkwa --- 4. Step 3: Run the Automated Installer Run the installer with root privileges: bash sudo ./scripts/install.sh The installer automatically: 1. Detects system architecture amd64 or arm64 . 2. Creates the dedicated non-login system user sparkwa . 3. Installs the single static binary to /usr/local/bin/sparkwa . 4. Creates protected directory /var/lib/sparkwa with mode 0700 . 5. Creates configuration directory /etc/sparkwa with mode 0750 . 6. Installs and enables the hardened sparkwa.service systemd unit. --- 5. Step 4: Configure Phone Numbers & Gmail Move your Google Cloud OAuth credentials into place: bash sudo mv /tmp/credentials.json /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json sudo chown root:sparkwa /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json sudo chmod 0600 /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json Edit the configuration file: bash sudo nano /etc/sparkwa/config.env Ensure the following variables are set: env E.164 phone numbers BOT PHONE NUMBER=\"+1234567890\" Number 1 the bot account AUTHORIZED PHONE NUMBER=\"+1987654321\" Number 2 the only allowed user Gmail Mailboxes BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS=\"bot-bridge@gmail.com\" SPARK GMAIL ADDRESS=\"personal-spark@gmail.com\" Paths DATA DIR=\"/var/lib/sparkwa\" GMAIL CREDENTIALS FILE=\"/etc/sparkwa/credentials.json\" GMAIL TOKEN FILE=\"/var/lib/sparkwa/token.json\" Timing GMAIL POLL INTERVAL=\"45s\" TASK TIMEOUT=\"15m\" LOG LEVEL=\"info\" Save and exit Ctrl+O , Enter , Ctrl+X . --- 6. Step 5: Authorize Gmail API OAuth 2.0 Run the Gmail authorization helper as the sparkwa service user: bash sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa gmail-auth --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env Follow the on-screen instructions see GMAIL OAUTH SETUP.md GMAIL OAUTH SETUP.md for details : - Either forward port 8085 ssh -L 8085:127.0.0.1:8085 user@your-vps-ip - Or paste the redirect code directly into the terminal prompt. --- 7. Step 6: Pair WhatsApp Number 1 Terminal QR Code Pair the WhatsApp account for Number 1 directly in your SSH terminal: bash sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa login --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env 1. An ASCII QR code will be displayed directly inside your SSH terminal. 2. Open WhatsApp on phone Number 1 . 3. Tap Settings Linked Devices Link a Device . 4. Point your camera at the SSH terminal QR code. 5. The terminal will output: text ✓ WhatsApp device pairing successful! ✓ WhatsApp pairing complete. Session saved in SQLite database. --- 8. Step 7: Run System Diagnostics Doctor Run the diagnostic check to ensure all subsystems are ready: bash sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa doctor --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env Verify that all critical checks report ✓ PASS : - Configuration Validation - Filesystem Permissions 0700 data dir, 0600 token & config - SQLite Database Integrity - WhatsApp Pairing Status - Gmail API Connectivity --- 9. Step 8: Start the systemd Background Service Start the service and enable it to persist across VPS reboots: bash sudo systemctl start sparkwa sudo systemctl status sparkwa Check live logs via journalctl : bash sudo journalctl -u sparkwa -f --- 10. Step 9: Send Your First WhatsApp Command From phone Number 2 , send a WhatsApp message to phone Number 1 : text /ping The bot will reply within seconds: text Pong! SparkWA is responsive. Timestamp: 2026-10-31T18:00:00Z UTC Next, test status: text /status Then submit your first task: text Please summarize my upcoming meetings for this week. SparkWA will return: text SparkWA SUBMITTED ID: d3b07384 Task: Please summarize my upcoming meetings for this week. Forwarded to Gemini Spark via Gmail monitor. Waiting for agent processing. Once your Gemini Spark agent processes the command, the result is forwarded back to your WhatsApp chat! --- 11. Maintenance and Useful Commands bash View live logs sudo journalctl -u sparkwa -f Check recent tasks and capability status sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa status --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env Stop service sudo systemctl stop sparkwa Restart service sudo systemctl restart sparkwa Create automated backup sudo /usr/local/bin/sparkwa-backup or /tmp/sparkwa/scripts/backup.sh "},{"title":"Connect Gemini Spark","description":"Configure the Gmail-triggered Spark workflow and return path.","path":"docs/spark-setup/","body":" Google Gemini Spark Integration Guide This guide details the exact procedure for connecting your existing Google Gemini Spark agent to SparkWA using Google's documented Gmail-triggered Task Schedules . --- 1. Architectural Overview WhatsApp User Number 2 │ ▼ SparkWA VPS │ ▼ Gmail API Bridge Mailbox BRIDGE GMAIL │ ▼ SPARKWA:REQ ID Email Command Spark-Connected Gmail Inbox SPARK GMAIL │ ▼ Event Monitor Trigger Gemini Spark Agent Autonomous Execution │ ▼ Output Channel Email Reply or Gmail Draft SparkWA Gmail Poller │ ▼ WhatsApp Message WhatsApp User Number 2 --- 2. Prerequisites in Google Account 1. Google Account with Spark Agent : - The Google Account SPARK GMAIL ADDRESS must have access to Gemini Spark / Google AI Workspace features. - Ensure Personal Intelligence and Gemini Apps Activity are enabled in your Google Account settings. 2. Connected Apps Enabled : - Open Gemini https://gemini.google.com . - Click Settings gear icon Extensions / Connected Apps . - Enable Google Workspace grants access to Gmail, Google Drive, Docs, and Calendar . --- 3. Creating the Gmail-Triggered Spark Schedule You configure Spark schedules conversationally or through Gemini's task schedule UI. Step-by-Step Configuration: 1. Open Gemini on your desktop or mobile browser. 2. In the prompt box, type the following schedule creation instruction: text Create a schedule that monitors my Gmail inbox. Whenever an email arrives from \"BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS\" with a subject containing \" SPARKWA:\", execute the command contained inside the email body following the SparkWA protocol. Replace BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS with your actual bridge email address, e.g. my-bridge-bot@gmail.com . 3. Gemini will present a schedule confirmation card showing: - Trigger : Incoming email matching from:BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS subject: SPARKWA: - Frequency : Automatic Event-based Gmail monitor - Task Action : Execute instructions and format output. 4. Click Confirm / Save Schedule . --- 4. Master Spark Task Prompt Copy-Paste Attach this prompt to your Gemini Spark custom instructions, Gem, or schedule task description. This ensures Spark operates 24/7 autonomously, never pauses to ask for email confirmation, never echoes command envelopes, and always delivers direct results: text You are an autonomous execution worker integrated with SparkWA. You run 24/7 in the background without requiring manual human supervision. You receive automated tasks via incoming emails with subjects formatted as: SPARKWA:REQUEST ID <Short Title The email body will follow this format: BEGIN SPARKWA COMMAND Request ID: <REQUEST ID Authorized sender: <MASKED PHONE Timestamp: <ISO8601 Command: <USER INSTRUCTION END SPARKWA COMMAND OPERATING PROTOCOL: 1. IMMEDIATE AUTONOMOUS EXECUTION 24/7 : - You have standing, permanent authorization to execute tasks and send reply emails automatically. - NEVER ask \"Should I send this email?\", \"Do you want me to reply?\", or \"Shall I proceed?\". - NEVER pause or wait for user confirmation to send the email response. Send the email reply directly and immediately to the sender. 2. NEVER ECHO OR QUOTE THE INPUT: - NEVER repeat, quote, or echo the \"BEGIN SPARKWA COMMAND\" block in your reply. - Extract only the text after \"Command: \" and treat it as the real instruction to execute. - If the command is a conversational greeting e.g., \"Hi\", \"Hello\" , reply directly and politely. - If the command is a query, answer it thoroughly. - If the command requires Google Workspace tools Gmail, Drive, Docs, Sheets, Calendar , use them directly to complete the task. 3. STRICT RESPONSE FORMAT: Always reply directly to the email thread using this exact structure: BEGIN SPARKWA RESPONSE Request ID: <REQUEST ID Status: SUCCESS Result: <YOUR DIRECT ANSWER, SUMMARY, OR OUTPUT END SPARKWA RESPONSE - Replace <REQUEST ID with the exact Request ID from the incoming email. - Put your actual answer or output after \"Result: \". - Do not include extra conversational text outside of this block. 4. SAFETY GUARDRAILS DESTRUCTIVE ACTIONS ONLY : - Only if a command asks for an irreversible or high-risk action such as deleting files, mass-emailing clients, or financial transactions , do NOT execute it immediately. - Instead, respond with: BEGIN SPARKWA RESPONSE Request ID: <REQUEST ID Status: APPROVAL REQUIRED Result: This action requires your confirmation: brief description . Reply YES to confirm. END SPARKWA RESPONSE --- 5. Output Paths & Feasibility Gate Google Gemini Spark operates in the background, but its return mechanisms depend on your Google Workspace settings. SparkWA supports two verified return channels: Output Path A: Direct Email Reply Recommended - Spark generates a reply email addressed to BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS containing BEGIN SPARKWA RESPONSE . - SparkWA's poller discovers this email via Gmail API within 45 seconds and forwards it to WhatsApp. Output Path B: Identifiable Draft - If your Gemini account policy restricts autonomous external outbound emails, instruct Spark to create a Draft in the Gmail account. - Set SPARK RESPONSE MODE=\"draft or email\" in /etc/sparkwa/config.env . - SparkWA checks for drafts matching SPARKWA:REQ ID and reads the result. Output Path C: Submission-Only Fallback - If your Gemini Spark setup executes tasks directly in Google Docs, Google Sheets, or Drive without creating emails or drafts, configure: env SPARK RESPONSE MODE=\"submission only\" - In this mode, SparkWA acknowledges task submission on WhatsApp and tracks the task as SUBMISSION ONLY . You inspect results directly in Google Workspace. --- 6. Read-Only Verification Test Before enabling destructive or complex tasks, perform this dedicated test: 1. On your VPS, run the diagnostic test command: bash sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa test-gmail --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env 2. Verify in your Spark Gmail inbox that an email was received with Subject: SPARKWA:test-... SparkWA Diagnostics Ping 3. Observe if Gemini Spark triggers and generates a response. 4. Send a WhatsApp message from Number 2 to Number 1: text /status Confirm that the capability state reports SPARK TRIGGER CONFIGURED or RESPONSE PATH VERIFIED . --- 7. Troubleshooting Spark Triggers Issue Likely Cause Resolution ------- -------------- ------------ Spark schedule does not trigger Filter mismatch Ensure the email Subject contains SPARKWA: with no typos in BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS . Spark triggers with long delay Background scheduling queue Google Workspace monitors poll on Google's internal schedule. Delays of 1–3 minutes can occur under high Google Cloud load. Spark does not send email reply Output permissions restricted Instruct Spark to create a Gmail draft instead, and set SPARK RESPONSE MODE=\"draft or email\" . Task shows APPROVAL REQUIRED Guardrail triggered Reply on WhatsApp with your confirmation e.g., \"Yes, proceed with task <ID \" . "},{"title":"Gmail OAuth setup","description":"Create Google Cloud credentials and authorize the bridge mailbox.","path":"docs/gmail-oauth/","body":" Google Gmail OAuth 2.0 Setup Guide SparkWA uses official Google Gmail APIs with least-privilege OAuth 2.0 scopes. It does not use deprecated Out-Of-Band OOB authentication, does not store Google account passwords, and never exposes public HTTP listeners. --- 1. Google Cloud Console Setup Step 1: Create or Select a GCP Project 1. Go to the Google Cloud Console https://console.cloud.google.com/ . 2. Click the project dropdown at the top and click New Project . 3. Name it SparkWA-Bridge and click Create . Step 2: Enable the Gmail API 1. In the search bar at the top, type Gmail API and select it from Marketplace. 2. Click Enable . Step 3: Configure the OAuth Consent Screen 1. In the left navigation menu, go to APIs & Services OAuth consent screen . 2. Select External or Internal if using Google Workspace organization and click Create . 3. Fill in the required fields: - App name : SparkWA - User support email : Your personal email. - Developer contact information : Your personal email. 4. Click Save and Continue . 5. On the Scopes page, click Add or Remove Scopes . Add the following minimum scopes: - https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.send Send messages on your behalf - https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly Read messages and drafts 6. Click Save and Continue . 7. On the Test Users page, click + Add Users and add: - Your BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS - Your personal email 8. Click Save and Continue . !IMPORTANT Testing Mode Note: In Google Cloud's \"Testing\" status, OAuth refresh tokens expire after 7 days unless your GCP project is moved to \"In Production\" status. To avoid re-authorizing weekly, click Publish App on the OAuth Consent Screen page even without full verification, published personal apps with personal scopes retain persistent refresh tokens . Step 4: Create OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials 1. Go to APIs & Services Credentials . 2. Click + Create Credentials OAuth client ID . 3. Select Application type : - Choose Desktop app recommended for loopback redirect OR Web application . - If choosing Web application , add Authorized redirect URI: http://127.0.0.1:8085/oauth/callback 4. Click Create . 5. Click Download JSON on the confirmation dialog. 6. Rename the downloaded file to credentials.json . --- 2. Deploying Credentials to VPS Copy credentials.json from your development machine to your VPS: bash scp credentials.json user@your-vps-ip:/tmp/credentials.json On the VPS, move it to the protected configuration directory: bash sudo mkdir -p /etc/sparkwa sudo mv /tmp/credentials.json /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json sudo chown root:sparkwa /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json sudo chmod 0600 /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json --- 3. Running Headless OAuth Authorization Because your Ubuntu VPS has no graphical interface or browser, SparkWA provides a secure loopback helper that coordinates with your desktop browser. Method 1: SSH Port Forwarding Recommended 1. Connect to your VPS with port 8085 forwarded to your local machine: bash ssh -L 8085:127.0.0.1:8085 user@your-vps-ip 2. Run the authorization command on the VPS: bash sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa gmail-auth --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env 3. SparkWA will print an authorization link: text Open this authorization URL in your browser: https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth?... 4. Copy the link and open it in your desktop browser. 5. Log in with your Bridge Gmail Account BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS , review permissions, and click Allow . 6. Google will redirect to http://127.0.0.1:8085/oauth/callback . Because port 8085 is forwarded via SSH, the request reaches your VPS loopback server automatically! 7. The terminal will display: text ✓ Received authorization code via loopback callback! ✓ Successfully saved OAuth token with refresh capabilities to /var/lib/sparkwa/token.json permissions 0600 ✓ Successfully verified Gmail API connection for: bot-bridge@gmail.com Method 2: Manual Terminal Paste Fallback If you cannot forward ports via SSH: 1. Open the URL printed by sparkwa gmail-auth in your desktop browser. 2. After clicking \"Allow\", your browser will attempt to open http://127.0.0.1:8085/oauth/callback?state=...&code=4/0A... . 3. Even though the browser shows \"Connection Refused\", simply copy the entire URL from the browser's address bar. 4. Paste that URL directly into the terminal prompt: text Auth Code or Redirect URL: http://127.0.0.1:8085/oauth/callback?code=4/0A... 5. SparkWA extracts the code and exchanges it for the token automatically. --- 4. Token Security - Stored at: /var/lib/sparkwa/token.json - Permissions: 0600 readable only by the sparkwa service account . - SparkWA automatically uses the refresh token to renew expired access tokens silently in the background. "},{"title":"Security and permissions","description":"Understand the allowlist, permissions, sandboxing, and approval boundaries.","path":"docs/security/","body":" SparkWA Security Policy & Architecture Security and operational reliability are primary design pillars of SparkWA . This document details the threat model, defensive controls, privilege separation, and disclosures. --- 1. Threat Model & Defensive Invariants Threat Impact Defensive Control in SparkWA -------- -------- ------------------------------ Unauthorized WhatsApp Senders Command injection, unauthorized AI task execution Strict single-user allowlist : Fail-closed verification matching normalized E.164 phone numbers. Unknown senders and unmapped LIDs are dropped without response. WhatsApp Group Message Ingestion Accidental command execution from shared chats Group messages dropped : Any message where evt.Info.IsGroup == true is silently discarded before parsing. Broadcast / Status Flood Resource exhaustion, queue poisoning Broadcasts ignored : Status announcements and broadcast lists are dropped immediately. Looping Self-Replies Infinite recursive command dispatch Self-messages dropped : evt.Info.IsFromMe == true messages are never processed as commands. Arbitrary Shell Execution Host compromise Zero shell execution : SparkWA contains no shell evaluation exec.Command or sh -c for chat input. All user messages are routed purely through structured API requests. Malicious Email Spoofing Fake Spark task results injected into WhatsApp Strict sender & correlation checks : Incoming emails must originate from the verified SPARK GMAIL ADDRESS and must match an active task's unique Request ID. Credential Theft on VPS Google account compromise Linux privilege isolation : Service runs as a dedicated non-root user sparkwa with restrictive 0600 file permissions on tokens and ProtectSystem=strict systemd sandboxing. --- 2. Principle of Least Privilege Dedicated Non-Root User SparkWA executes under the dedicated Linux system user sparkwa /usr/sbin/nologin . It does not run as root. Filesystem Permissions - /etc/sparkwa/config.env : Mode 0600 , owned by sparkwa:sparkwa . - /etc/sparkwa/credentials.json : Mode 0600 , owned by root:sparkwa . - /var/lib/sparkwa/token.json : Mode 0600 , owned by sparkwa:sparkwa . - /var/lib/sparkwa/ .db : Mode 0600 , located inside directory mode 0700 . Systemd Hardening Flags The installed service unit enforces Linux kernel sandboxing: - ProtectSystem=strict : The entire OS filesystem is mounted read-only except /var/lib/sparkwa . - ProtectHome=yes : /home , /root , and /run/user are completely invisible to the process. - NoNewPrivileges=yes : Prevents privilege escalation. - PrivateTmp=yes : Isolated temporary directories. - PrivateDevices=yes : Access to physical hardware devices is blocked. Minimal Gmail Scopes SparkWA requests only the minimum required Gmail API scopes: 1. https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.send : Allows dispatching command emails to the Spark mailbox. 2. https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly : Allows reading responses and drafts. It does not request broad account access or delete permissions. --- 3. Preserving Gemini Spark Approval Guardrails Google Gemini Spark includes safety guardrails requiring user confirmation before executing sensitive external actions e.g., deleting documents, making outbound transactions, sending emails . SparkWA will NEVER attempt to bypass, spoof, or auto-confirm an approval prompt. When Spark flags an action as requiring approval either via Status: APPROVAL REQUIRED or phrases like \"Do you want me to proceed with...\" : 1. The task transitions to APPROVAL REQUIRED . 2. SparkWA sends an alert to WhatsApp Number 2 displaying the exact confirmation request. 3. Execution remains paused until you review the request and explicitly reply on WhatsApp. --- 4. WhatsApp Automation Disclosures & Risk Management !WARNING WhatsApp Terms of Service Disclosure: WhatsApp's Terms of Service restrict unauthorized automation on personal consumer accounts. The underlying multi-device library whatsmeow simulates WhatsApp Web Linked Devices. To mitigate account risk: 1. Use a dedicated SIM for Phone Number 1 the bot account . Never use your primary personal phone number as the bot account. 2. SparkWA uses conservative rate limits, delays between multi-part chunks, and low message frequency. 3. Senders other than Number 2 are ignored without replies, avoiding spam flags from unknown contacts. "},{"title":"Troubleshooting","description":"Diagnose WhatsApp, Gmail, SQLite, and service issues.","path":"docs/troubleshooting/","body":" SparkWA Troubleshooting Guide This guide covers common issues, root causes, and resolutions for SparkWA. --- 1. Diagnostics First sparkwa doctor Whenever something is not behaving as expected, stop the service and run the diagnostic doctor: bash sudo systemctl stop sparkwa sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa doctor --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env The output will pinpoint which subsystem is failing. --- 2. WhatsApp Issues Issue A: Terminal QR Code Looks Garbled - Cause: Your SSH terminal client is using a non-UTF8 font or improper character encoding. - Resolution: - In your terminal emulator PuTTY, iTerm2, Windows Terminal, GNOME Terminal , set the character encoding to UTF-8 . - Increase terminal font size or widen the window so half-block ASCII characters align properly. Issue B: \"Session logged out or revoked\" - Cause: WhatsApp Web was manually disconnected from the phone, or WhatsApp rotated session security keys. - Resolution: 1. Stop the background service: bash sudo systemctl stop sparkwa 2. Re-pair the device: bash sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa login --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env 3. Scan the new QR code with phone Number 1. 4. Restart the service: bash sudo systemctl start sparkwa Issue C: Bot Ignores WhatsApp Messages - Cause: 1. The sender is not Phone Number 2 AUTHORIZED PHONE NUMBER . 2. The message was sent in a group chat groups are ignored . 3. The phone number in config.env does not match the exact international E.164 format. - Resolution: - Verify that AUTHORIZED PHONE NUMBER in /etc/sparkwa/config.env starts with + and includes the country code e.g. +1987654321 . - Run sudo journalctl -u sparkwa -f while sending a message. If the sender is unauthorized, a debug log will show that the sender was ignored. --- 3. Gmail and OAuth Issues Issue A: \"failed to refresh token: oauth2: cannot fetch token: 400 Bad Request\" - Cause: - The Google Cloud OAuth consent screen is in \"Testing\" mode, and 7 days have passed since authorization. In Testing mode, Google expires refresh tokens after 7 days. - Resolution: 1. Go to Google Cloud Console OAuth Consent Screen https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials/consent . 2. Click Publish App to switch from \"Testing\" to \"In Production\". 3. Re-run authorization on your VPS: bash sudo systemctl stop sparkwa sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa gmail-auth --config /etc/sparkwa/config.env sudo systemctl start sparkwa Issue B: \"authenticated account does not match configured bridge address\" - Cause: - You logged into Google during sparkwa gmail-auth using your personal or Spark account instead of BRIDGE GMAIL ADDRESS . - Resolution: - Run sparkwa gmail-auth again and ensure you select the bridge account when granting permissions in your browser. --- 4. SQLite Database & Lock Conflicts Issue: \"database is locked\" or \"cannot start client while service is running\" - Cause: - Both the sparkwa.service background daemon and a manual CLI command e.g. sparkwa login or sparkwa run are trying to access the same SQLite database files simultaneously. - Resolution: - Always stop the background systemd service before running interactive CLI commands that mutate sessions: bash sudo systemctl stop sparkwa perform command sudo -u sparkwa sparkwa login sudo systemctl start sparkwa --- 5. Gemini Spark Trigger Latency Issue: Spark takes 1–3 minutes to process commands - Cause: - Google's Gmail Monitor schedules do not execute synchronously on email arrival; Google's backend worker polls inbox filters periodically. - SparkWA's default poll interval is 45 seconds to stay well below Gmail API quotas. - Expected Timing: - Normal round-trip time between command submission on WhatsApp and final result delivery is between 45 and 180 seconds . --- 6. Systemd Service Failures Inspect the systemd exit code and full log stream: bash sudo systemctl status sparkwa.service sudo journalctl -u sparkwa -n 50 --no-pager Common causes: - File permissions: Ensure /var/lib/sparkwa is owned by sparkwa:sparkwa sudo chown -R sparkwa:sparkwa /var/lib/sparkwa . - Missing config: Ensure /etc/sparkwa/config.env exists and is readable by the sparkwa group 0600 or 0640 . "},{"title":"SparkWA overview","description":"Architecture, installation, and links to the official docs.","path":"","body":"WhatsApp to Gemini Spark Go Gmail bridge architecture installation private self-hosted"},{"title":"Complete reference","description":"Full original single-page SparkWA documentation, commands, examples, FAQ, and diagrams.","path":"reference/complete-guide.html","body":"SparkWA WhatsApp Gemini Spark setup installation pipeline commands examples security FAQ"}];